From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 03:59:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(task): add a strict, dependency-free format for a saved
 task

Eighth slice: the half of "resume after the app was closed" that needs no
Android, so it can actually be tested here.

core/task/TaskProgressCodec:
- A line-based format with a version header and one line per step. Hand-written
  rather than kotlinx.serialization because the file has to stay readable by a
  resumed process across app versions, must not change shape when a data class
  gains a field, and should be diffable in a bug report.
- The reader is strict: a wrong magic, a newer version, an oversized file
  (64 KiB), more than 200 steps, a malformed line, a bad value or a duplicate
  step id are all refused with a named DecodeRejection - a corrupt file never
  half-loads. Unknown fields are ignored *and reported*, so a newer writer does
  not break an older reader.
- Values are escaped (backslash, pipe, newline, carriage return), so a failure
  message can never break the record, and the message is redacted on the way in
  and on the way out - a step that failed while quoting a token cannot persist
  it.
- A decoded task feeds straight into TaskResume, which the tests check.

Two real bugs the tests caught, both fixed: an empty timestamp column was
treated as an error, which rejected every step that had not started or finished
yet; and the failure column was redacted but never escaped, so a message with a
newline or a pipe corrupted the file.

Tests: TaskProgressCodecTest (17).
